  <h1>What makes fresh graduates unhireable and how do you fix it?</h1>
  <p class="answer-capsule">Fresh graduates are unhireable when they have zero proof of output: no artifact a recruiter can evaluate. The fix is a scored work sample that proves capability before the first interview.</p>

  <div class="tldr">
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://www.shrm.org/topics-tools/news/talent-acquisition/skills-gap-college-graduates" target="_blank">72% of employers say new graduates lack practical skills</a>, per 2024 SHRM data.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/features/bul-124-2-262.pdf" target="_blank">Work-sample tests predict job performance better than unstructured interviews</a>, according to Schmidt and Hunter's landmark validity research, confirmed by <a href="https://psycnet.apa.org/record/2023-38400-001" target="_blank">Sackett et al. 2022 meta-analysis in Personnel Psychology</a>.</li>
      <li>A Middle East university pilot, Spring 2026, showed per-submission feedback drove repeat engagement over generic career advice.</li>
      <li>The gap is signal, not talent. Build recruiter-visible receipts and the hiring dynamic shifts.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>Why do recruiters say fresh graduates are unhireable?</h2>
    <p>Recruiters do not hate new graduates. They hate guessing. When a single junior role draws <a href="https://www.jobvite.com/resources/blog/recruiting-metrics-benchmark-report/" target="_blank">between 100 and 300 applications on average</a>, a recruiter spends less than a minute scanning the entire first pass. According to a <a href="https://www.theladders.com/static/images/basicSite/pdfs/TheLadders-EyeTracking-StudyC2.pdf" target="_blank">2018 eye-tracking study by TheLadders</a>, the median review time per résumé is roughly 7.4 seconds.</p>
    <p>A degree from a known school gets you sorted into a maybe pile. It does not get you hired.</p>
    <p>The real problem surfaces in screening interviews. Recruiters report that fresh graduates struggle to describe work they have actually done. They discuss coursework. They discuss group projects where their contribution is unclear. They have no artifact a recruiter can evaluate. This is what employers call the <a href="https://www.shrm.org/topics-tools/news/talent-acquisition/skills-gap-college-graduates" target="_blank">skills gap</a>. In a 2024 SHRM survey, 72% of HR professionals said entry-level candidates lack applied abilities.</p>
    <p>Think of it as a restaurant where diners cannot see the menu and the kitchen cannot see the diners. Students think the credential is the signal. Employers think the credential is noise. Both sides lose.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How does the skills gap data actually look?</h2>
    <p>NACE (National Association of Colleges and Employers) surveys employers every hiring cycle. In <a href="https://www.naceweb.org/talent-acquisition/benchmarks/" target="_blank">2024 only 53% of entry-level hires met all stated competency expectations</a> on day one per NACE benchmarks. Employers wanted written communication, problem solving, and teamwork. They got theoretical knowledge.</p>
    <p>BLS data tells a similar story. <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/youth.pdf" target="_blank">Youth unemployment for 20-24 year-olds in early 2025 ran at roughly 7.9%</a>. That is more than double the <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm" target="_blank">national average of roughly 4.1%</a>. The gap suggests students are not failing. The evaluation system is broken.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What did a real pilot reveal about fixing this?</h2>
    <p>A Middle East university pilot, Spring 2026, tested a different model. Instead of assessments first, students picked a scored task immediately. They completed marketing briefs, product requirements, and design sprints. Each submission received per-submission feedback instead of generic career advice.</p>
    <p>The pattern that emerged aligned with what the pilot team observed in other education products. Students wanted to do something real within 90 seconds. The heavy onboarding scaffolding that works in education products did not work here. When the team collapsed the intro to a single "pick your first task" screen, more students reached their first scored submission.</p>
    <p>Per-submission feedback also drove every return visit. Students said they returned to the platform specifically for the feedback on their submission. Generic skill assessments were ignored. Students wanted to know what they did right and what to fix.</p>
    <p>One student said they would rather submit something rough and get real feedback than read another article about marketing. Another said they did not know they could do this kind of work without a job. The feedback was the engagement loop.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How do scored work samples change hiring?</h2>
    <p>Schmidt and Hunter's foundational research on personnel selection validity found that <a href="https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/features/bul-124-2-262.pdf" target="_blank">work-sample tests correlate at about 0.54 with job performance</a>. Sackett et al. confirmed in a <a href="https://psycnet.apa.org/record/2023-38400-001" target="_blank">2022 Personnel Psychology meta-analysis</a> that work samples remain among the top predictors of job performance. In plain language: work samples predict success about 40% better than unstructured interviews (0.38 correlation). Both outperform years of education (0.10 correlation).</p>
    <p>This is not a new idea. It is an old idea that modern hiring forgot because work samples are expensive to administer at scale.</p>
    <p>Simulators change the economics. Instead of a recruiter designing a custom test, students complete calibrated tasks against a rubric. The output is scored. The recruiter gets a sortable signal without designing a single question.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Signal quality</th><th>Time invested</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Résumé screen</td><td>Low: self-reported</td><td>Under a minute total</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Phone screen</td><td>Medium: verbal claim</td><td>30 minutes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Assessment test</td><td>High: but generic</td><td>2-3 hours</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scored simulator task</td><td>High: job-identical output</td><td>4-6 hours</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The simulator stage asks for more time than a résumé. It produces evidence that an assessment test cannot match because the output is real work: a marketing brief, a product spec, a design file. A recruiter evaluates it the same way they would evaluate a portfolio.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How do you actually become hireable instead of just credentialed?</h2>
    <p>The fix has three parts.</p>
    <p>First, do real work in public. Not a class project. Not a tutorial clone. A task that looks like the work you want to do. If you want a marketing role, write a campaign brief for a real company. If you want a product role, write a PRD for an existing product you use. Publish it. Score it. Get feedback.</p>
    <p>Second, collect feedback that names your specific gaps. Generic advice is cheap. Specific feedback on your actual output is hard to find. When you get it, treat it as your primary asset. It tells you exactly what a hiring manager would flag.</p>
    <p>Third, make that evidence recruiter-visible. A portfolio is good but passive. You want a signal that travels to recruiters without you applying. That is what a scored platform does. Recruiters sort by percentile. They contact you.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.joinzero.ai">zero is built for exactly this</a>. Students complete real tasks for real companies. Their scored output is visible to recruiters. It is free for students.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What makes someone unhireable even with internships?</h2>
    <p>Internships help but they are not a guarantee. Many internships produce no artifact you can show a recruiter. If your internship was photocopying, coffee runs, and shadowing, it is barely better than no internship.</p>
    <p>The students who break through are the ones who leave every experience with receipts. They do not say "I interned at X company." They say "I built Y feature and it increased Z metric by W percent." The second statement requires proof. The first does not.</p>
    <p>See how <a href="/learn/how-does-performance-based-hiring-work-for-students">performance-based hiring works for students</a> for the full mechanics.</p>
  </section>

